<HelpLayout title="Badger on your watch" description="Log sets, run your rest timer and finish a workout from a Wear OS watch, with Badger on your Android phone.">
  <h1>Badger on your watch</h1>

  <p>On a Wear OS watch, Badger is a remote for the workout running on your Android phone. Start the workout on the phone, then log sets and run your rest from your wrist. The watch stores nothing.</p>

  <hr />

  <h2 id="set-up">Install it</h2>
  <p>You need Wear OS 3 or newer, paired with an Android phone that has Badger. Install Badger on the phone first. Then open the Play Store on the watch, search for <strong>Badger</strong> and install it.</p>

  <h2 id="start">Start a workout</h2>
  <p>Start the workout on your phone, then open Badger on the watch or tap its tile. While the workout runs, a chip on the watch face shows your current set or the rest.</p>

  <h2 id="log-a-set">Log a set</h2>
  <p>The watch shows your next set, filled in from the plan. Tap <strong>Log set</strong>. When the phone confirms it, the watch shows <strong>Logged</strong>.</p>
  <p>To change the set first:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Turn the crown or rotary bezel to change the weight. It moves by that exercise's own step on the phone.</li>
    <li>Tap <strong>-</strong> or <strong>+</strong> to change the reps.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Logging from the watch works wherever a set is waiting to be ticked: routine days, <a href="/help/logging/#logging-mode">Plan, then tick</a>, and any time the workout is open on the phone's training screen. In <strong>Log as you go</strong>, the watch shows the current exercise and the rest, and you add sets on the phone.</p>

  <h2 id="rest">Run your rest</h2>
  <p>The rest countdown runs on the watch, with <strong>-15 s</strong>, <strong>Skip</strong> and <strong>+15 s</strong>, the same buttons as the <a href="/help/logging/#lock-screen">lock screen</a>. The watch buzzes once when the rest ends. While the watch app is running, the phone's own rest alert stays quiet.</p>

  <h2 id="exercises">Move between exercises</h2>
  <p>Swipe up from the set screen to see your exercises with their done and total sets. Tap one to jump to it. Supersets advance in the same order as on the phone.</p>
  <p>Timed sets show the countdown or stopwatch with <strong>Stop</strong>, which fills in the duration as it does on the <a href="/help/logging/#set-timer">phone</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="finish">Finish the workout</h2>
  <p>After the last planned set, the watch shows the <strong>Plan done</strong> card with <strong>Not yet</strong> and <strong>Finish workout</strong>. It follows your <a href="/help/settings/#after-last-planned-set">After the last planned set</a> setting.</p>
  <p>If some sets are not ticked, the watch says <strong>Finish on your phone</strong>, because the phone asks what to do with them. After you finish, the watch shows a short summary. The full summary is on the phone.</p>

  <h2 id="no-phone">When the phone can't be reached</h2>
  <p>If the watch can't reach the phone, nothing is logged. The watch tells you and offers <strong>Try again</strong>. Log the set again once the two are connected.</p>

  <h2 id="heart-rate">Heart rate</h2>
  <p>Badger's watch app doesn't read heart rate. Run your watch's own exercise app alongside Badger for that. The phone reads heart rate per set from Health Connect: see <a href="/help/body/#heart-rate">Heart rate on your sets</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="reference">What to know</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Needs:</strong> Wear OS 3 or newer, paired with an Android phone that has Badger.</li>
    <li><strong>Weight unit:</strong> follows the phone's logging unit for that exercise, kg or lb.</li>
    <li><strong>On the phone only:</strong> starting a workout, adding or swapping exercises, notes and plate math.</li>
  </ul>
</HelpLayout>
